Difference between final, finally, finalize?	final: keyword to restrict. finally: code block that always executes after try-catch. finalize(): method called by Garbage Collector before destroying an object.
import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;

public class FinallyDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        
        // --- Scenario 1: No Exception Occurs ---
        System.out.println("--- Scenario 1: No Exception ---");
        performOperation(10, 5);
        
        // --- Scenario 2: Exception Occurs and is Caught ---
        System.out.println("\n--- Scenario 2: Exception is Caught ---");
        performOperation(10, 0); 

        // --- Scenario 3: Exception Occurs and is NOT Caught ---
        // We demonstrate this conceptually, as an uncaught runtime exception would stop the program.
        // A simple example like division by zero is shown here, though typically 
        // finally is used for Checked Exceptions like IO.
        System.out.println("\n--- Scenario 3: Exception is Uncaught (or propagated) ---");
        try {
            // Simulating an unexpected exception type (e.g., NullPointerException)
            String s = null;
            s.length(); 
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println("CATCH: Caught only ArithmeticException."); 
        } finally {
            // This runs even though NullPointerException wasn't explicitly caught.
            System.out.println("FINALLY: Cleanup task completed (runs even if exception not caught).");
        }
    }

    public static void performOperation(int num1, int num2) {
        FileWriter writer = null;
        try {
            System.out.println("TRY: Attempting operation...");
            
            // This line throws an ArithmeticException if num2 is 0
            int result = num1 / num2; 
            System.out.println("TRY: Result = " + result); 
            
            // Simulating another action that needs cleanup
            writer = new FileWriter(new File("output.txt"));
            writer.write("Operation successful.");
            
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            // This block executes ONLY if ArithmeticException is thrown
            System.err.println("CATCH: Error caught: Division by zero is not allowed.");
            
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // Handles potential file writing errors
            System.err.println("CATCH: File writing error.");
            
        } finally {
            // This block ALWAYS executes
            System.out.println("FINALLY: Closing resources (always executes).");
            if (writer != null) {
                try {
                    writer.close();
                    // NOTE: You must have a nested try-catch here for the close() operation
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    System.err.println("FINALLY Error: Failed to close writer.");
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

Final keyword:
•	Final Variables: A variable declared final becomes a constant, meaning its value cannot be changed or reassigned after it is initialized. For reference variables (objects or arrays), the reference itself cannot be changed to point to a new object, but the internal state of the object/array can still be modified (unless the object itself is immutable, like String).
o	Example: final int MAX_VALUE = 100;
When applied to a variable, final means the variable's value cannot be changed after it is initialized. 
•	Primitive Types (e.g., int, boolean): The stored value is a constant. Once initialized, the int variable x will always hold that exact number.
•	Object References (Non-primitive types, e.g., String, ArrayList): The reference to the object is constant, meaning it will always point to the same memory location (the same object instance).
o	The object's state can still be modified if the object is mutable. For example, a final ArrayList can have elements added or removed, but the variable cannot be reassigned to a different ArrayList object.
o	For an object to be truly immutable, its class must be designed carefully (like the built-in String class) with all its fields also being final and immutable. 


      import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class MutableObjectReferenceExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // The reference 'finalList' is constant. It points to one specific ArrayList object.
        final List<String> finalList = new ArrayList<>();

        // The object's state can be modified because ArrayList is mutable:
        finalList.add("Apple"); // This is allowed
        finalList.add("Banana"); // This is allowed
        System.out.println("After adding elements: " + finalList); // Output: After adding elements: [Apple, Banana]

        // Modifying an element is also allowed:
        finalList.set(0, "Apricot");
        System.out.println("After setting an element: " + finalList); // Output: After setting an element: [Apricot, Banana]

        // Removing elements is allowed:
        finalList.remove("Banana");
        System.out.println("After removing an element: " + finalList); // Output: After removing an element: [Apricot]

        // This would cause a compilation error:
        // finalList = new ArrayList<>(); // Error: Cannot assign a value to final variable 'finalList'
    }
}

public class ImmutableObjectReferenceExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // The reference 'finalString' is constant. It points to the String "Hello".
        final String finalString = "Hello";

        // This line creates a NEW String object "Hello World" in memory.
        // It does not change the original "Hello" object.
        // The result is not assigned back to 'finalString', so it's a lost operation.
        finalString.concat(" World");

        System.out.println("Original String: " + finalString); // Output: Original String: Hello (Still "Hello")

        // This would cause a compilation error if we tried to reassign it:
        // finalString = "Goodbye"; // Error: Cannot assign a value to final variable 'finalString'
    }
}

•	Final Methods: A method declared final cannot be overridden by any subclasses. This ensures that the method's behavior remains consistent throughout the inheritance hierarchy, which is useful for maintaining critical or secure logic.
o	Example: public final void display() { ... }
•	Final Classes: A class declared final cannot be extended or inherited by any other class. This prevents any other class from altering its design or behavior through subclassing. System classes such as java.lang.String and java.lang.Math are examples of final classes.
o	Example: public final class FinalClass { ... } 
