OOPS Concept	Core Concepts of Java OOP
Class: A class is a blueprint or template from which individual objects are created. It defines the properties (attributes/fields) and behaviors (methods) that all objects of that type will have.
Object: An object is an instance of a class, representing a real-life entity. Each object has its own state (data) and can perform actions through its methods. 
The Four Pillars of OOP
Encapsulation: This involves bundling data and methods within a class, controlling access to the data, typically by making variables private and using public getter and setter methods. This helps in data hiding and security.
Inheritance: Allows a new class (subclass) to acquire properties and behaviors from an existing class (superclass), promoting code reuse and establishing an "is-a" relationship. In Java, this is done using the extends keyword.
class Animal { // Parent class
    void eat() { System.out.println("Eating..."); }
}
class Dog extends Animal { // Child class
    void bark() { System.out.println("Barking..."); }
}


Polymorphism: Meaning "many forms," polymorphism in Java enables a single method or object to have different behaviors depending on the context. This is achieved through Method Overloading (same method name, different parameters) and Method Overriding (subclass providing a specific implementation of a superclass method).
•	Compile-time Polymorphism (Overloading): Multiple methods in the same class with the same name but different parameters.
•	Runtime Polymorphism (Overriding): A subclass provides its own specific implementation for a method already defined in its parent class.
o	Example: A Shape class has a draw() method. Both Circle and Square classes override it to draw their respective shapes.
 
Example: When you use a remote control, you press a button to change channels (the interface), but you don't need to know how the internal circuitry processes that signal (the implementation)

Abstraction: This principle focuses on showing only essential features while hiding implementation details. It allows focusing on what an object does rather than how it does it and is achieved in Java through abstract classes and interfaces.
Java 8 features	•	Lambda Expressions: Allows treating functionality as a method argument or code as data. They provide a concise way to implement Single Abstract Method (SAM) interfaces.
o	Example: (a, b) -> a + b; replaces the need for bulky anonymous inner classes.
•	Functional Interfaces: An interface with exactly one abstract method. Java 8 introduced a standard set in the java.util.function package, such as Predicate, Consumer, and Function.
•	Stream API: A powerful abstraction for processing sequences of elements (like collections) in a declarative way. It supports intermediate (filter, map) and terminal (forEach, collect) operations.
