Lamda expressions	Syntax Overview
The basic syntax consists of three parts: a parameter list, the arrow operator (->), and a body. 
•	Parameters: Can be zero (), one (p), or multiple (a, b). Type declaration is optional.
•	Arrow (->): Separates the parameters from the logic.
•	Body: Can be a single expression (no braces or return needed) or a block of code {} (requires return for non-void methods). 
________________________________________
Examples of Lambda Expressions
1. Zero Parameters
Used for interfaces like Runnable where the method takes no arguments. 
java
// Implementation of Runnable
Runnable task = () -> System.out.println("Task is running!");
task.run();
Use code with caution.
 
2. Single Parameter
If there is only one parameter, parentheses are optional. 
java
// Doubling a number
Function<Integer, Integer> doubleIt = n -> n * 2;
System.out.println(doubleIt.apply(5)); // Output: 10
Use code with caution.
 
3. Multiple Parameters
Parentheses are required for more than one parameter. 
java
// Adding two numbers
BinaryOperator<Integer> add = (a, b) -> a + b;
System.out.println(add.apply(10, 20)); // Output: 30
Use code with caution.
 
4. Iterating Collections
Lambdas are commonly used with the forEach method for clean iteration. 
java
List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
names.forEach(name -> System.out.println(name));
Use code with caution.
 
5. Filtering with Streams
Used within the Stream API to filter data based on conditions. 
java
List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6);
numbers.stream()
       .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0) // Keep even numbers
       .forEach(System.out::println);
Use code with caution.
 
