Streams	Key Operations
Stream operations are divided into two categories: 
•	Intermediate Operations (return a Stream):
o	filter(): Selects elements matching a given condition.
o	map(): Transforms each element into another value.
o	sorted(): Sorts the elements of a stream.
o	distinct(): Removes duplicate elements.
o	limit(): Truncates the stream to a maximum size.
o	skip(): Discards the first n elements.
•	Terminal Operations (produce a result or side-effect):
o	collect(): Gathers the processed elements into a Collection (e.g., a List or Set).
o	forEach(): Performs an action for each element.
o	reduce(): Combines all elements into a single result (e.g., calculating a sum).
o	count(): Returns the number of elements in the stream.
o	anyMatch(), allMatch(), noneMatch(): Check if elements match a given predicate. 

Abstract class and Interface	1. Abstract Class
An abstract class is a partially implemented class that cannot be instantiated directly. It is used when you have a group of closely related classes that share a common identity and logic. 
•	Structure:
o	Methods: Can have both abstract methods (no body) and concrete methods (with implementation).
o	Fields: Can have instance variables (non-static, non-final) that maintain the state of an object.
o	Constructors: Can have constructors to initialize common state for subclasses.
o	Access Modifiers: Methods can be public, protected, or private.
•	Behavior in Subclass:
o	Compulsory Implementation: A subclass must override all abstract methods unless it is also declared as an abstract class.
o	Single Inheritance: A class can only extend one abstract class.
o	Method Overriding: Subclasses can override concrete methods to change their behavior or use them as-is. 
2. Interface
An interface is a contract that defines what a class can do, rather than what it is. It is ideal for defining common behaviors among unrelated classes. 
•	Structure:
o	Methods: By default, methods are public and abstract. Since Java 8+, it can also have default and static methods with implementations. Java 9+ introduced private methods for internal reuse.
o	Fields: Variables are implicitly public static final (constants).
o	No Constructors: Interfaces cannot have constructors.
•	Behavior in Subclass:
o	Implementation Contract: A class "implements" an interface and must provide bodies for all its abstract methods.
o	Multiple Implementation: A class can implement multiple interfaces, allowing it to take on many behaviors.
o	Default Methods: If an interface provides a default method, the subclass can use it without overriding it, which helps evolve APIs without breaking old code. 
Feature 	Abstract Class	Interface
Inheritance	Single inheritance (extends)	Multiple inheritance (implements)
State	Can have instance variables	Can only have constants
Methods	Abstract, concrete, final, static	Abstract, default, static, private
Constructors	Yes	No
Access	Any modifier (private, protected, etc.)	Implicitly public (except private methods)
When to use which?
•	Use an Abstract Class when you want to provide a common base with shared code and state for a tightly related family of classes (e.g., Animal -> Dog, Cat).
•	Use an Interface when you want to define a specific capability that many different types of objects can have (e.g., Flyable -> Bird, Airplane, Drone)




