Inheritance examples	// 1. Parent (Super) Class
class Vehicle {
    protected String brand = "Ford"; // Accessible to subclasses

    public void startEngine() {
        System.out.println("Engine starting...");
    }
}

// 2. Child (Sub) Class
class Car extends Vehicle {
    private String model = "Mustang";

    // Subclass specific method
    public void displayDetails() {
        // Can access parent variable directly if not private
        System.out.println("Brand: " + brand + ", Model: " + model);
    }

    @Override
    public void startEngine() {
        // Calling the parent version of the method using 'super'
        super.startEngine(); 
        System.out.println("Car specifically is ready to race!");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 3. Creating an Object of the subclass
        Car myCar = new Car();

        // 4. Method Calls
        myCar.startEngine();    // Calls overridden method in subclass
        myCar.displayDetails(); // Calls subclass-specific method
    }
}

Key Mechanisms Explained
•	Object Creation: When you use new Car(), Java first invokes the constructor of the parent (Vehicle) and then the child (Car) to ensure all inherited properties are initialized.
•	Method Calls:
o	Inherited Methods: If a method is defined only in the parent, calling it from a child object executes the parent's code.
o	Overridden Methods: If the child redefines a parent method, the child's version is called by default.
•	The super Keyword:
o	Inside Methods: Used to call the original parent version of a method that has been overridden.
o	Inside Constructors: Used (as super()) to explicitly call a parent constructor, particularly if the parent requires specific arguments.
•	Parent References: A parent class reference variable can hold a child object (e.g., Vehicle v = new Car();). However, it can generally only access methods and variables defined in the Vehicle class unless the object is cast back to a Car. 

Example2: Accessing Only Parent Members
Even if the actual object is a Car, the Vehicle reference limits your access to only those methods and variables defined in Vehicle.
class Vehicle {
    String type = "Generic Vehicle";
    void move() { System.out.println("Vehicle is moving"); }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    String model = "Sedan"; // Child-specific variable
    void openTrunk() { System.out.println("Trunk is open"); } // Child-specific method
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Upcasting: Parent reference holds Child object
        Vehicle v = new Car(); 

        v.move();           // ✅ Works: Defined in Vehicle
        // v.openTrunk();   // ❌ Compile Error: Vehicle does not have openTrunk()
        // System.out.println(v.model); // ❌ Compile Error: Vehicle does not have model
    }
}

Example3: Runtime Polymorphism (Overriding)
While the parent reference cannot access new child methods, if the child overrides a parent method, the child's version will execute at runtime. 

class Vehicle {
    void start() { System.out.println("Generic start"); }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    @Override
    void start() { System.out.println("Car starts with a roar!"); }
}


Vehicle v = new Car();
v.start(); // Outputs: "Car starts with a roar!" (Dynamic Method Dispatch)

3.  Downcasting: Accessing Child-Specific Members
Vehicle v = new Car();

// Explicit Downcasting to access Car-specific features
if (v instanceof Car) { // Best practice: Check type first to avoid ClassCastException
    Car myCar = (Car) v;
    myCar.openTrunk();  // ✅ Now works after casting
    System.out.println("Model: " + myCar.model); // ✅ Now accessible
}



