1. Page Object Model (POM)
POM is the industry standard for Selenium testing. It separates the application's UI (locators and interactions) from the test logic by creating a class for each web page. 
•	Example: If you have a login page, you create a LoginPage class containing elements like the username field and login button.
•	Benefit: If a UI element changes, you only update the locator in one class rather than in every test script. 
2. Factory Design Pattern
This pattern is used to centralize and simplify the creation of WebDriver instances (e.g., Chrome, Firefox, Safari). 
•	Example: A WebDriverFactory class takes a string input (like "chrome") and returns the corresponding driver object.
•	Benefit: Decouples the test scripts from the specific browser initialization logic, allowing you to switch browsers easily via configuration. 
3. Singleton Design Pattern
The Singleton pattern ensures that only one instance of a WebDriver exists throughout the test run. 
•	Example: A class with a private constructor and a static method like getInstance() that returns the existing driver or creates a new one if it doesn't exist.
•	Benefit: Prevents accidental instantiation of multiple browser windows, saving system resources. 
java
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.firefox.FirefoxDriver;
// ... potentially other driver imports

public class DriverManager {

    // A ThreadLocal variable to store the WebDriver instance for each thread
    private static final ThreadLocal<WebDriver> DRIVER_THREAD_LOCAL = new ThreadLocal<>();

    /**
     * Initializes the WebDriver for the current thread.
     * @param browserType The type of browser to initialize (e.g., "chrome", "firefox").
     */
    public static void initializeDriver(String browserType) {
        if (DRIVER_THREAD_LOCAL.get() == null) {
            WebDriver driver;
            switch (browserType.toLowerCase()) {
                case "chrome":
                    driver = new ChromeDriver();
                    break;
                case "firefox":
                    driver = new FirefoxDriver();
                    break;
                // Add more cases for other browsers as needed
                default:
                    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported browser type: " + browserType);
            }
            DRIVER_THREAD_LOCAL.set(driver);
            // Optional: Add some implicit waits or window maximization here
            driver.manage().window().maximize();
        }
    }

    /**
     * Gets the WebDriver instance for the current thread.
     * @return The WebDriver instance.
     */
    public static WebDriver getDriver() {
        if (DRIVER_THREAD_LOCAL.get() == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Driver not initialized for this thread. Call initializeDriver() first.");
        }
        return DRIVER_THREAD_LOCAL.get();
    }

    /**
     * Quits the WebDriver and removes the instance from ThreadLocal storage for the current thread.
     */
    public static void quitDriver() {
        WebDriver driver = DRIVER_THREAD_LOCAL.get();
        if (driver != null) {
            driver.quit();
            DRIVER_THREAD_LOCAL.remove(); // Essential to clear the thread's reference
        }
    }
}
