Selenim grid	1. Prerequisites
Ensure you have Java installed and download the standalone Selenium Server JAR file (e.g., selenium-server-4.x.x.jar) from the official Selenium website.
2. Setting Up the Grid (Command Line)
You need to run two separate commands in different command prompts/terminals.
A. Start the Hub
The hub is the central point that manages test sessions.
bash
java -jar selenium-server-4.x.x.jar hub
Use code with caution.
By default, the hub runs on http://localhost:4444/.
B. Start a Node
The node is the machine where the actual browser instance will run. It registers with the hub.
bash
java -jar selenium-server-4.x.x.jar node --hub http://localhost:4444/
Use code with caution.
You can start multiple nodes on different machines or the same machine to run tests in parallel.
3. Configuring the Java Code
In your automation framework (e.g., a TestNG test using the Page Object Model), you need to switch from a local WebDriver (like ChromeDriver) to a RemoteWebDriver that points to your hub's URL.
java
import org.openqa.selenium.Platform;
import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.remote.DesiredCapabilities;
import org.openqa.selenium.remote.RemoteWebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions;

import java.net.MalformedURLException;
import java.net.URL;

public class RemoteDriverFactory {

    public static WebDriver getRemoteDriver(String browserName) throws MalformedURLException {
        WebDriver driver;
        URL hubUrl = new URL("http://localhost:4444/");
        
        if (browserName.equalsIgnoreCase("chrome")) {
            ChromeOptions options = new ChromeOptions();
            // Optional capabilities, like running headless
            // options.addArguments("--headless"); 
            
            driver = new RemoteWebDriver(hubUrl, options);
            
        } else if (browserName.equalsIgnoreCase("firefox")) {
            // Use FirefoxOptions for configuration
            DesiredCapabilities cap = new DesiredCapabilities();
            cap.setBrowserName("firefox");
            cap.setPlatform(Platform.LINUX); // Or WINDOWS/MAC
            
            driver = new RemoteWebDriver(hubUrl, cap);
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Browser " + browserName + " not supported.");
        }
        
        return driver;
    }
}
Use code with caution.
